## Upgrade example

Change `image` (and `talos_version` in the data source) to trigger an in-place OS upgrade:

```terraform
data "talos_machine_configuration" "this" {
  # ...
  talos_version = "v1.10.0"
}

resource "talos_machine" "this" {
  # ...
  image = "ghcr.io/siderolabs/installer:v1.10.0"
}
```

## Upgrading multiple nodes safely

When managing a multi-node cluster, upgrading all nodes in parallel risks losing etcd quorum. Use `depends_on` to chain upgrades sequentially, so each node is fully back before the next one starts:

```terraform
resource "talos_machine" "cp0" {
  node             = "10.5.0.1"
  image            = "ghcr.io/siderolabs/installer:v1.10.0"
  drain_on_upgrade = true
  # ...
}

resource "talos_machine" "cp1" {
  depends_on       = [talos_machine.cp0]
  node             = "10.5.0.2"
  image            = "ghcr.io/siderolabs/installer:v1.10.0"
  drain_on_upgrade = true
  # ...
}

resource "talos_machine" "cp2" {
  depends_on       = [talos_machine.cp1]
  node             = "10.5.0.3"
  image            = "ghcr.io/siderolabs/installer:v1.10.0"
  drain_on_upgrade = true
  # ...
}
```

Alternatively, use `terraform apply -parallelism=1` to force all resource operations to run one at a time without modifying `depends_on`.

## Kubernetes component image management

`talos_machine` does **not** apply changes to the five Kubernetes component image fields:

- `machine.kubelet.image`
- `cluster.apiServer.image`
- `cluster.controllerManager.image`
- `cluster.scheduler.image`
- `cluster.proxy.image`

These fields are owned by [`talos_cluster`](cluster.md), which drives [Talos's `upgrade-k8s` procedure](https://docs.siderolabs.com/kubernetes-guides/advanced-guides/upgrading-kubernetes/). That procedure pre-pulls images, upgrades static pods component-by-component and node-by-node with health gating, updates the kube-proxy DaemonSet, upgrades kubelet sequentially, and re-applies bootstrap manifests. Applying these fields in parallel from `talos_machine` would bypass that safety procedure.

Practical consequences:

- Bumping `kubernetes_version` in `talos_machine_configuration` alone has no effect on running nodes. To upgrade Kubernetes, change `talos_cluster.kubernetes_version`.
- Drift in these five fields (e.g. after `upgrade-k8s` has patched the on-disk config) is not detected by `talos_machine` and does not trigger a re-apply.
- The `kubernetes_version` you set in `talos_machine_configuration` still matters at **bootstrap** — new nodes added later (scale-up) will come up at that version. Keep it in sync with `talos_cluster.kubernetes_version` so new nodes don't bootstrap at a stale version.

If you use `talos_machine` without `talos_cluster`, run `talosctl upgrade-k8s` manually to upgrade Kubernetes.

